# Cloudflare Pages headers — Shikkha24
#
# Source: docs/security.md §5 (security headers + CSP).
#
# TWO CSP PROFILES (docs/security.md §5): a strict one for static / editorial
# pages, and a relaxed one for ad-bearing and interactive pages, because AdSense
# needs inline scripts and dynamic script injection.
#
# ⚠️ Cloudflare Pages does NOT override a duplicated header — **it joins the
# values with a comma**, and a comma-joined CSP is enforced as two policies, so
# the strict `script-src 'self'` would still block AdSense. A more specific rule
# therefore has to DETACH the inherited value first (`! Content-Security-Policy`)
# and then set its own. Verified against the Pages headers documentation
# 2026-09-12.
#
# ⚠️ **S12 check:** confirm in production that an ad-bearing page returns exactly
# ONE Content-Security-Policy header. If the detach does not take effect the
# header will contain a comma, AdSense will be blocked, and this must be resolved
# by moving the CSP into a Pages Function instead.
#
# ⚠️ **S12 check (added with the S11 URL change):** confirm the article rule's
# `/:category/:id/:slug` placeholder actually matches in production. Request a
# real article URL and assert the response carries the RELAXED policy (it must
# name `pagead2.googlesyndication.com`). A placeholder that fails to match fails
# **silently** — the page still returns 200, just under the strict policy, and
# AdSense is blocked on every article with no error anywhere. If placeholders do
# not behave as documented, the fallback is a Pages Function that sets the CSP
# per request (see the detach note above).
#
# ⚠️ **`cms.shikkha24.com` is a PLACEHOLDER hostname.** It is the CMS origin AND
# the media origin (media moved off R2 to the DianaHost disk on 2026-09-12 —
# `.sps/plan.md` §"Scope amendment"). A CSP cannot be templated, so this literal
# host MUST match the production `PUBLIC_CMS_ORIGIN` and `PUBLIC_MEDIA_BASE_URL`
# exactly. It is currently a placeholder because the CMS hostname is still
# pending DNS.
#
# S9 changes, recorded so the diff is not a mystery later:
#   • `img-src` / `media-src` now name the CMS host, not the retired R2 host —
#     every article image would otherwise be blocked in production
#   • `connect-src` now names the CMS host in the STRICT profile too, because the
#     newsletter signup sits in the footer of every page and the comment,
#     citizen and bookmark surfaces post to the CMS from editorial pages
#   • `/article/*` gains `frame-src` for the click-to-load video embeds
#   • `/gallery` and `/gallery/*` were REMOVED from the relaxed list: the gallery
#     carries no ad slot (none of the eight approved `AD_POSITIONS` is a gallery
#     slot) and no inline script, so the strict profile is sufficient and
#     tighter. If a gallery ad slot is ever approved, add both rules back.
#
# S11 change (2026-09-13), recorded for the same reason:
#   • The article rule moved from `/article/*` to `/:category/:id/:slug`, following
#     the URL architecture change (`docs/seo-plan.md` §2). This is NOT cosmetic —
#     `/article/*` would have stopped matching every article page, silently
#     dropping them to the strict profile and blocking AdSense site-wide. See the
#     placeholder warning above.
#   • `/article/:slug` is now a redirect stub and deliberately keeps the strict
#     profile (no script, no ad slot).

/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=()
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  # `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` is required by Astro's inline stylesheets
  # (`build.inlineStylesheets: 'auto'` in astro.config.mjs). It is the only
  # relaxation in this profile — scripts stay strict.
  #
  # `connect-src` names the CMS because the newsletter signup form is in the
  # footer of every page and posts to it. The form is not rendered at all unless
  # `PUBLIC_CMS_ORIGIN` is set, so an unconfigured deploy makes no such request —
  # but the policy has to allow it for the configured one.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://cms.shikkha24.com; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com; media-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Relaxed profile — routes that carry ad slots, or whose islands need a
# third-party frame.
#
#   /                     homepage — 4 ad slots + the poll + newsletter
#   /:category/:id/:slug  article  — 3 ad slots + comments + bookmarks + video embeds
#   /category/*           category — 1 ad slot
#   /live/*               live blog — 1 ad slot + the refresh island
#   /quiz, /quiz/*        quiz     — 2 ad slots
#
# ⚠️ The article rule uses PLACEHOLDERS, not a splat — and that is load-bearing.
#
# `_headers` allows only ONE splat per URL, and a splat greedily matches `/`
# (Cloudflare Pages docs, verified 2026-09-13). The old `/article/*` rule worked
# only because every article shared a literal `/article/` prefix. After the S11
# URL change (`docs/seo-plan.md` §2) an article lives at `/:category/:id/:slug`
# and has NO common prefix — `/admission/10/some-slug` is indistinguishable from
# the category listing `/category/admission` by prefix alone.
#
# A placeholder matches exactly one path segment, so `/:category/:id/:slug`
# matches the three-segment article path and nothing else. **Do not "simplify"
# this to a splat:** `/*/*/*` is invalid (one splat per rule) and `/*` would
# relax the CSP for the entire site — silently re-opening the AdSense-blocking
# bug that S8 fixed site-wide.
#
# Every other route keeps the strict policy, which is the point of two profiles.
# That now includes `/article/:slug` — since S11 those are 200-OK redirect stubs
# with no script and no ad slot, so the strict profile is correct and tighter.
# It also includes `/gallery` (no ad slot, no inline script), `/bookmarks` (no
# CMS call at all — the list is browser-local) and `/citizen` (posts to the CMS,
# which the strict `connect-src` already allows).
#
# The AdSense loader only injects the library when a real AdSense unit exists, and
# the islands only call the CMS when the reader taps something — so today these
# routes still make zero third-party requests. The relaxation costs nothing until
# the features are actually used.
#
# `frame-src` on the article rule is for the video embed, which is click-to-load:
# the iframe is created only after the reader taps play, so no third-party request
# is made on page load. `youtube-nocookie.com` is used deliberately — it sets no
# tracking cookie until playback.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/
  ! Content-Security-Policy
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: https://cms.shikkha24.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.gstatic.com; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com; connect-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.google.com https://*.doubleclick.net; media-src 'self' https://cms.shikkha24.com; frame-src https://*.googlesyndication.com https://*.doubleclick.net https://*.google.com https://googleads.g.doubleclick.net; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests

